A||N||8||Eriamjh||New bushings... Arms still move.||||1||
Z||000000||Eriamjh||12-01-2001||10:03 PM||||OK, I just replaced all the front bushings with poly and I have to wait for my front springs to arrive in the mail.<P>Here's the prob:  I have the upper and lower arms bolted in the car awaiting the springs.  The bolts are all new, are fully installed but are not torqued down.  The upper control arms can still move because the bolt can move within the "tube" that the bolt pivots within.  Is this normal?  I used the exact same bolts as the car came with, at least I THINK I did.<P>12MM right?  Or are the uppers thicker?  Should I be using a 3/8 bolt?  I still have time to get a bigger bolt to fill in the sleeve, but not much longer.<P>Thanks for all the help!<p>[This message has been edited by Eriamjh (edited 12-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||2birds||12-01-2001||10:17 PM||||I used all of the original mounting bolts when I did mine (only had to replace one on the rear that was a bit too corroded).  No play in any of mine.  I used Prothane bushings from the Fiero Store, and I torqued everything to the OEM specs as laid out in the Haynes manual.  The one I replaced was a 12mm, though.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Indiana_resto_guy||12-02-2001||02:24 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Eriamjh:<BR><B>OK, I just replace all the front bushings with poly and I have to wait for my front springs to arrive in the mail.<P>here's the prob:  <P>The bolts are all new, are fully installed but are not torqued down.  The upper control arms can still move because the bolt can move within the "tube" that the bolt pivots within.  <P>I used the exact same bolts as the car cam with, at least I THINK I did.<P>12MM right?  Or are the uppers thicker?  Should I be using a 3/8 bolt?  I still have time to get a bigger bolt to fill in the sleeve, but not much longer.<P>Thanks for all the help!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You should have some washers on the outside of the bolt heads and nuts for 84-87. The mounting points may be bent out or flared as well.<BR>If your talking about an 88, you should have at least a couple of spacers usually on each side of the sleeve.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Indiana_resto_guy||12-02-2001||02:25 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Indiana_resto_guy:<BR><B> You should have some washers on the outside of the bolt heads and nuts for 84-87. The mounting points may be bent out or flared as well.<BR>If your talking about an 88, you should have at least a couple of spacers usually on each side of the sleeve.<P>How did you size your bolts?<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Fierowrecker||12-02-2001||04:30 AM||||Hey Eriamjh!<BR>Look at the setup again...<BR>You should have a spacer about a quarter of an inch thick between the tube and bushing on both sides...<BR>And yes, they will be loose till you torque them down...<BR>You will want to recheck the torque after a couple of hundred miles too, as things settle in...<BR>crash...<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Eriamjh||12-02-2001||08:08 AM||||Sorry.  '87 Coupe.<P>Right now everything is not tightened down.  There is play between the upper bolts and the tube they go through.  I have the washers and spacers in the exact same places that they were when I disassembled it.<P>I used 12MM grade 10.9 bolts but I didn't check to see for sure if the uppers used them.  I still have one bolt that survived removal (didn't reuse it) that I'll check with calipers tomorrow at work.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||neohio||12-02-2001||10:00 AM||||You should have washers with the bolt kit(that is if you got a bolt kit) the washers are supposed to take up any slack and used for adjusting the camber..i think camber but don't quote me on that..I ordered the Moog bolt kit and the bolt is a 12mm 10.9 grade||||reg||1
Z||000007||Eriamjh||12-02-2001||03:15 PM||||Let me clarify further:  There is inboard/outboard movement.  I think it's between the tube and the bolt.<P>I have new washers installed, but have not torqued anything down yet.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Eriamjh||12-02-2001||06:05 PM||||I figured it out.  Bolt to sleeve movement is normal when loose.<P>Notice the sleeve is serrated on the edges (as are the inner factory bushing sleeves)?  when you torque it together, the serrations dig into the washers and grab the upper arm preventing any movement.<P>I didn't buy my upper bolts from the Fiero store, but instead got them from a bolt shop.  Because of the digging action of the serrations, should my new washers be grade 10.9 like my bolts or a bit softer (like grade 8 or 5) so the serrations can dig in?  I think I need softer washers.<P>And the poly bushing sleeves are not serrated.  This obviously is not a problem.<P>OK, I just inspected the old washers and it looks like there are matching serrations already pressed or stamped into the washers and that they don't actually get "dug" into by the sleeve serrations.  It all makes sense now!<p>[This message has been edited by Eriamjh (edited 12-02-2001).]||||reg||1
